GOAL: make `series.progress.itemStyle.color: 'auto'` (and the per-data-item `data[i].progress.itemStyle.color`) resolve to the `axisLine.lineStyle.color` stop that corresponds to the data value — exactly the way `pointer.itemStyle.color: 'auto'`, `splitLine.lineStyle.color: 'auto'` and `axisTick.lineStyle.color: 'auto'` already do in the same view.

=== HOW 'auto' IS RESOLVED TODAY (precedent to copy) ===

`getColor` is built once in `_renderMain` and passed down to `_renderTicks`, `_renderTitleAndDetail`, `_renderPointer`.

/workspace/src/chart/gauge/GaugeView.ts:166-181
```ts
        const getColor = function (percent: number) {
            // Less than 0
            if (percent <= 0) {
                return colorList[0][1];
            }
            let i;
            for (i = 0; i < colorList.length; i++) {
                if (colorList[i][0] >= percent
                    && (i === 0 ? 0 : colorList[i - 1][0]) < percent
                ) {
                    return colorList[i][1];
                }
            }
            // More than 1
            return colorList[i - 1][1];
        };
```
(`colorList` = `seriesModel.get(['axisLine', 'lineStyle', 'color'])`, read in `render()` at GaugeView.ts:90 and passed to `_renderMain` at :94.)

Existing 'auto' sites: GaugeView.ts:261-265 (splitLine `stroke === 'auto'`), :349-353 (axisTick `stroke === 'auto'`), and the pointer, which is the exact template for the new code:

/workspace/src/chart/gauge/GaugeView.ts:549-556
```ts
                    pointer.setStyle(itemModel.getModel(['pointer', 'itemStyle']).getItemStyle());


                    if (pointer.style.fill === 'auto') {
